## Root cause

The hang is a Motion/interconnect deadlock caused by an incomplete squelch handler for **local (same-slice) Share Input Scan** nodes, in `src/backend/executor/nodeShareInputScan.c`.

**The chain:**

1. `ExecLimit` (`src/backend/executor/nodeLimit.c:245-264`), once the limit is satisfied, calls `ExecSquelchNode(node)` on itself (line 260) — unconditionally except when `expect_rescan` is set. This recurses down through the entire subtree feeding the Limit.
2. If that subtree contains an `Append`/`Sequence`/`MergeAppend`, their squelch handlers unconditionally squelch **every** branch, including branches that were **never executed** because an earlier branch alone already satisfied the LIMIT:
   - `ExecSquelchAppend` — `src/backend/executor/nodeAppend.c:761-768`: `for (i = 0; i < node->as_nplans; i++) ExecSquelchNode(node->appendplans[i]);`
   - `ExecSquelchSequence` — `src/backend/executor/nodeSequence.c:162-167`: same pattern over `node->subplans[i]`.
3. If one of those never-executed branches contains a **local** `ShareInputScan` (`sisc->cross_slice == false` — e.g. a CTE/shared subquery referenced only in that slice), `ExecSquelchNode` dispatches to `ExecSquelchShareInputScan` (`nodeShareInputScan.c:620-675`):

```c
if (sisc->cross_slice && node->ref)
{
    if (currentSliceId == sisc->producer_slice_id || estate->es_plannedstmt->numSlices == 1)
    {
        /* producer: force materialization if not ready, so cross-slice readers don't hang */
        if (!local_state->ready)
            init_tuplestore_state(node);
    }
    else
    {
        /* consumer: notify producer */
        ...
    }
}
```

For a **local** share, `sisc->cross_slice` is false and `node->ref` is always `NULL` (set in `ExecInitShareInputScan`, `nodeShareInputScan.c:499-513`: `sisstate->ref = node->cross_slice ? get_shareinput_reference(...) : NULL;`). So the entire body above is skipped — `ExecSquelchShareInputScan` does **nothing** but `ExecClearTuple`. Crucially, it also **deliberately does not recurse into the child** (comment at `nodeShareInputScan.c:630-641`: "its subtree may still need to be executed... don't recurse in this case").

That reasoning assumes some *other* sharer of the same local share will still execute the child later. But if this squelched instance is the one holding the real child (`local_state->childState`, set by whichever `ShareInputScanState` had a non-NULL `outerPlan` — see `ExecInitShareInputScan`, `nodeShareInputScan.c:438-440,494-495`), and no other sharer of that share_id ever gets a chance to run (because it, too, lives only in branches that the LIMIT/Append bypassed), then **nobody ever calls `init_tuplestore_state`** for that share. Its child subplan is never executed and never squelched.

If that child subplan starts with a receiving Motion (perfectly legal — a "local" SISC just means all *readers* are in one slice; the shared subplan it drains can still pull from a different sending slice across a Motion), the sending-side Motion on another QE blocks forever waiting for this receiver to either consume or explicitly squelch — exactly the class of deadlock `ExecSquelchNode` exists to prevent (see the doc comment at `execAmi.c:686-711`), except SISC's own squelch handler reintroduces it for the local-share case.

Contrast this with the **cross-slice producer** case, which already has the correct defensive logic: "if we haven't materialized the tuplestore yet, we need to do it now, even though we won't need the data... otherwise readers will hang waiting for us forever" (`nodeShareInputScan.c:646-657`). That fix was never extended to local shares — apparently an oversight, not a deliberate design choice, since eager materialization is just as safe for local shares (any later sharer that legitimately still wants to read this share simply finds `local_state->ready == true` and reuses the tuplestore via the "another local reader" path in `init_tuplestore_state`, `nodeShareInputScan.c:324-332`).

**Why it's intermittent:** it only manifests when (a) the plan has a `LIMIT` above an `Append`/`Sequence`/`MergeAppend`, (b) one branch contains a local Share Input Scan whose real child pulls across a Motion, and (c) the LIMIT happens to be satisfied entirely from *other* branches before that branch is ever touched. Whether (c) holds depends on branch order, row counts, and data skew across segments — the same query can sail through when the shared branch happens to get pulled at least once, and hang when it doesn't.

## Proposed fix

In `ExecSquelchShareInputScan` (`src/backend/executor/nodeShareInputScan.c:620-675`), add a branch for local shares mirroring the existing cross-slice-producer logic:

```c
void
ExecSquelchShareInputScan(ShareInputScanState *node)
{
	EState	   *estate = node->ss.ps.state;
	ShareInputScan *sisc = (ShareInputScan *) node->ss.ps.plan;
	shareinput_local_state *local_state = node->local_state;

	ExecClearTuple(node->ss.ps.ps_ResultTupleSlot);

	if (sisc->cross_slice && node->ref)
	{
		/* ... existing cross-slice logic, unchanged ... */
	}
	else if (!sisc->cross_slice && !local_state->ready)
	{
		/*
		 * Local share whose tuplestore was never materialized. If no other
		 * sharer of this share ever executes (e.g. this was the only
		 * occurrence, living in an Append/Sequence branch that got skipped
		 * once an enclosing LIMIT was satisfied elsewhere), nobody will ever
		 * drain local_state->childState. If that child contains a receiving
		 * Motion, its sender blocks forever. Materialize eagerly instead --
		 * safe even if another sharer does end up reading it later, since
		 * init_tuplestore_state() is idempotent on local_state->ready.
		 */
		init_tuplestore_state(node);
	}
}
```

This brings local shares to parity with the cross-slice producer path, which already handles the identical hazard.

**Trade-off:** this forces full execution of a shared subplan the first time *any* of its sharers is squelched without having run, even in cases where the branch really was abandoned and nobody would ever have needed the data — costing possibly-wasted work. That's the same trade-off already accepted for cross-slice producers, and correctness (no deadlock) should dominate. If the extra cost matters, a tighter version can be built by extending the existing `nsharers`/`ndone` bookkeeping (currently only counted for cross-slice consumers, `nodeShareInputScan.c:490-492`) to local shares too, and only force-materializing when the *last* live local sharer is being squelched — but that adds complexity for a performance-only benefit.

**Suggested regression test:** a plan with `LIMIT` over an `Append` (or `UNION ALL`) where one branch is a local CTE/shared-subquery reference behind a redistribute/broadcast Motion, and the other branch alone can satisfy the limit — run repeatedly (or pin via the existing `SIMPLE_FAULT_INJECTOR`/`Debug_shareinput_xslice` machinery already in the file) to force the "shared branch never touched" ordering deterministically, rather than relying on it happening probabilistically.

Want me to implement this change and add a regression test under `contrib/zdb/regress` (or `src/test/isolation2`, since this is core executor behavior, not ZDB-specific)?

**Root cause:** `zdb_get_metainfo_for_file` / `_for_drop_flag` / `_for_visimap` are global introspection UDFs — they scan *all* FDB keys of that kind cluster-wide, not just the ones for your table. A bare `select count(*) from zdb_get_metainfo_for_file(...)` will also pick up tombstone/segfile/visimap/drop-flag keys left behind by whatever other tests in the schedule ran before yours (or are running concurrently, since the schedule shares one cluster + FDB instance). That's exactly why it passes standalone but flakes in the full run — no other test's leftover keys are around to pollute the global scan.

**Fix:** capture your table's `relfilenode` into a helper table *before* the `DROP`, then join every introspection query against it instead of taking a raw `count(*)`. This repo already has the pattern in `contrib/zdb/regress/sql/backup_and_gc/setup_backup.sql`:

```sql
-- before DROP TABLE, record the relfilenode
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS relfilenode_record_for_drop_tbl(relname text, relfilenode oid);
INSERT INTO relfilenode_record_for_drop_tbl
  SELECT 'your_tbl', relfilenode FROM pg_class WHERE relname = 'your_tbl';

DROP TABLE your_tbl;

-- then scope every introspection query to that relfilenode
SELECT count(*) FROM
(
    SELECT tablespace, dbid, relationid, xmin FROM zdb_get_metainfo_for_file(2, <space>, 'S')
) a
JOIN
(
    SELECT relfilenode FROM relfilenode_record_for_drop_tbl WHERE relname = 'your_tbl'
) b ON a.relationid = b.relfilenode;
```

Same join pattern applies to `zdb_get_metainfo_for_drop_flag` (with `'D'`) and `zdb_get_metainfo_for_visimap` (with `'V'`). You can either inline this join in your test's `.sql` file, or reuse/adapt the `get_meta_file_count` / `get_meta_visimap_count` / `get_meta_drop_flag_count` plpgsql helper functions already defined in `setup_backup.sql` if your schedule loads that setup file.

Also worth checking in teardown: drop your `relfilenode_record_for_drop_tbl` rows (or use a distinctive table name) so you don't collide with other tests reusing the same helper table name, per the "Table-name collisions" guidance in CLAUDE.md.
